  "notes": "Lao (Laotian). Tai-Kadai abugida closely related to Thai script. 6-tone language. Consonants in three classes (low/mid/high) interact with tone marks. Inherent vowel is /o/ (short). Simplified compared to Thai: no ช/ซ/ถ/ท distinctions -- Lao merged many Thai letters. Vowel signs are written before, above, below, or after the consonant they belong to (Wikipedia, 'Lao script'); several are discontinuous circumfixes (e.g. ເ-ືອ /ɯa/). The base grapheme table used a placeholder dotted-circle (◌) to mark dependent-vowel position in the original skeleton; those keys never matched real text (the tokenizer does substring matching, and ◌ never appears in Lao text), so every postposed and preposed vowel silently fell through to the consonant's bare inherent /o/ -- the root cause of the near-total vowel collapse found against the kaikki gold set (PER 0.7225). Two further mechanical issues compound this: (1) Lao/Thai dependent vowel signs are Unicode category Lo (spacing letters), not Mn/Mc combining marks, so the engine's inherent-vowel-cancellation test in phonetok.py (_supplies_vowel) never recognizes a bare postposed vowel key as cancelling the preceding consonant's inherent vowel -- confirmed by testing a plain two-token fix in isolation (still gives koaː for kaː). The only data-layer fix under the current tokenizer is to enumerate consonant+vowel (and consonant+vowel+coda) combinations as single multigraph tokens, which is what this spec now does for every base consonant. (2) Preposed vowel signs (ເ ແ ໂ ໃ ໄ) are written before the consonant they belong to but pronounced after it; the tokenizer has no mechanism to reorder tokens, so this is fixed the same way (a vowel+consonant multigraph per consonant). Residual limitation, not fixable at the data layer: syllable-final glide consonants (ວ /w/, ຍ /ɲ/ used as codas) still pick up a spurious inherent vowel of their own when they are not covered by an explicit CVC multigraph, since the tokenizer has no notion of syllable position (onset vs. coda) for a bare consonant letter -- confirmed present in Thai too (กาว 'glue' currently transcribes koɡaːwo, not kaːw). Tone is not emitted at all: tone_inventory is descriptive metadata only (per the vi/nan convention) -- no tone-mark graphemes exist and no code path synthesizes a tone diacritic from consonant class + tone mark + syllable weight, so the Chao tone letters in the kaikki gold transcriptions have no engine-side counterpart to compare against. These fixes lower kaikki-gold PER from 0.7225 to 0.5183 (n=2305); most residual error is the untranscribed tone (unavoidable, see above) plus two further gaps left for future work rather than hacked around here: (a) the same syllable-final-consonant-keeps-its-inherent-vowel issue described above also affects plain non-glide codas (e.g. ດ/t̚, ບ/p̚, ງ/ŋ, ນ/n as syllable-final stops/nasals), which is the same tokenizer limitation, just far more combinations to enumerate than the glide-coda cases fixed here; (b) the silent leading-consonant construct (Thai/Lao 'ห นำ': ຫ + a low-class sonorant, e.g. precomposed ໜ/ໝ or the digraph spelling, makes the sonorant behave as high tone class while ຫ itself is not pronounced) is not modeled at all -- affected words surface an extra spurious /h/+inherent-vowel syllable (e.g. ໜ່ວຍ, ຫຍັງ). Both are real, scoped-out gaps, not silently-wrong guesses.",
